/// Who did what to which row, kept.
///
/// ─── This is NOT `@@log(audit)`, and the difference is the point ──────────
///
/// `@@log(audit)` records a WRITE — litestone's own trail, into a declared
/// `database audit` block, with `@encrypted`/`@guarded`/`@secret` columns
/// redacted (Invariant 7). It is free, it is complete, and it can only ever see
/// writes.
///
/// This model is for the events that are NOT writes to a model. A sign-in is
/// not a write to `User`. A FAILED sign-in is not a write at all. An export, a
/// permission grant that was refused, a webhook that was replayed — nothing the
/// logger can see names the actor or the attempt.
///
/// `db.$audit({ operation, model?, records?, actorId?, meta? })` is the other
/// half and needs no model: it writes into litestone's own trail by hand. Reach
/// for THIS model instead when the trail has to be a first-class read — a
/// screen with a filter and a window over it, a retention different from the
/// logger's, or rows an app queries rather than an operator greps. basecamp has
/// the one instance, and that is exactly why: `verify:screens` renders it, 50
/// rows with a cursor.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `before` and `after`.** `diff` is one column, because the pair invites
/// storing whole rows and a whole row contains the columns Invariant 7 exists to
/// redact. A diff is authored by the writer, who knows what may be recorded.
///
/// **No `ip` or `userAgent`.** They belong to a REQUEST and most rows here are
/// not written by one — a job, a cron fire, a replay. An app that wants them
/// puts them in `meta`, where their absence is not a lie.
enum ActorKind { user api_key system }

model AuditEvent {
  id           String     @id @default(uuid())

  /// The tenant, where there is one. Nullable and NOT a relation: this file
  /// declares no tenant model. Wire it in your own schema, and note the
  /// nullability is load-bearing rather than lazy — an event about the
  /// installation itself (a sysadmin acting across tenants, a boot-time
  /// migration) belongs to no tenant.
  ///
  /// Under `strategy row` a nullable tenant column means a row belonging to no
  /// tenant, which every tenant then reads. That is correct here and is the one
  /// place it is: the gate below is 5 for read and the rows are the operator's.
  /// Say it out loud with `@@tenant(none)` and an explicit policy rather than
  /// leaving the column nullable and hoping.
  workspaceId  String?

  /// Who. Nullable because the actor may be the application itself — a job, a
  /// cron fire, `createApp({ system })` resolving to nobody. A plain column, not
  /// a relation: an actor may be a deleted user, and a foreign key would either
  /// refuse the write or take the history with the row.
  actorId      String?

  /// What KIND of actor, because an id alone cannot say. A user, a machine, an
  /// API key, the system. Defaulted rather than nullable so a row always
  /// answers — and an ENUM, because that list is closed and known: the writer
  /// is one hook, so a value outside the set is a bug rather than a new case.
  /// Compare `subjectType` below, which is the opposite call for the opposite
  /// reason.
  actorType    ActorKind  @default(user)

  /// The verb. A string and not an enum, for `Notification.type`'s reason: the
  /// set lives in application code and an enum makes every new one a migration.
  action       String

  /// Which row it was done to — the polymorphic subject, a type name and a key,
  /// because it points at a different model per row and a foreign key names one
  /// table.
  ///
  /// **This spelling is the catalogue's preference for anything new.**
  /// `Notification` in this tree spells the same idea
  /// `contextType`/`contextId`, because its package chose first. Two spellings,
  /// one concept, and neither is wrong — see references/README.md.
  ///
  /// Required here where a notification's is optional: an audit row with no
  /// subject records that something happened to nothing.
  ///
  /// ─── Why this one is a String and `actorType` is not ────────────────────
  ///
  /// A pair like this has no foreign key, so the ONE thing it can still be told
  /// is which models it may name — an `enum`, which emits a table CHECK (so a
  /// migration, a seed and `asSystem()` are held to it too) and reaches the
  /// browser as a set a picker renders. Do that wherever the set is knowable.
  /// Measured across seven published schemas, most are: ERPNext declares
  /// `party_type` CLOSED twice and leaves the same set unconstrained sixteen
  /// times in the same codebase.
  ///
  /// **This is the exemption.** An audit trail names whatever it was pointed
  /// at — in practice the service that wrote the row — so the set grows with
  /// every service and an enum would refuse the first row a new one writes.
  /// `fli check`'s `polymorphic-subject` names that case and a
  /// `check-baseline.json` entry is how an app says it means it.
  subjectType  String
  subjectId    String

  /// What changed, authored by the writer rather than captured. Nullable: a
  /// sign-in changes nothing and still belongs here.
  ///
  /// **Nothing redacts this.** It is yours, so do not put a secret in it — the
  /// same rule `$audit`'s own `meta` carries.
  diff         Json?

  createdAt    DateTime   @default(now())

  /// Read 5 · create 8 · update 9 · delete 9.
  ///
  /// Append-only, and LOCKED rather than SYSTEM for the two that would edit
  /// history: a trail `asSystem()` can rewrite is not a trail. 9 is how that is
  /// spelt for every caller including the application itself.
  ///
  /// Read at 5 is an administrator. Lower it with a policy if the product shows
  /// people their own activity; do not lower the gate.
  @@gate("5.8.9.9")

  /// Two reads and they are different questions: the operator's feed for one
  /// tenant, and *everything that ever happened to this row*.
  @@index([workspaceId, createdAt])
  @@index([subjectType, subjectId])
}
